
---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const repository = "https://github.com/Shohan093/Distributed-RAG";

const results = [
  {
    transition: "V3 → V1",
    naive: "0.000",
    compensated: "0.772",
    oracle: "0.791",
    recovery: "97.6%",
  },
  {
    transition: "V3 → V2",
    naive: "0.001",
    compensated: "0.810",
    oracle: "0.866",
    recovery: "93.5%",
  },
];

const methods = [
  {
    name: "L0 · Translation",
    description:
      "Shifts each query by a global mean-difference vector. It was inexpensive, but retrieval quality remained close to zero.",
    outcome: "Baseline",
  },
  {
    name: "L1 · Orthogonal rotation",
    description:
      "Learns a global rotation from paired calibration embeddings using orthogonal Procrustes alignment.",
    outcome: "Best overall",
  },
  {
    name: "L2 · Rotation + local correction",
    description:
      "Adds cluster-specific residual corrections to L1. It did not improve retrieval quality in these experiments.",
    outcome: "No improvement",
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Drift-RAG | Distributed Retrieval Systems"
  description="A distributed retrieval system that uses query-side embedding alignment to recover retrieval quality across embedding-model transitions."
>
  <main class="container section project-detail">
    <nav class="breadcrumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/projects/">Projects</a>
      <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <span aria-current="page">Drift-RAG</span>
    </nav>

    <header class="project-hero">
      <p class="eyebrow">PROJECT 01 · DISTRIBUTED SYSTEMS</p>

      <h1 class="project-title">
        Drift-RAG:
        <span>Retrieval across changing embedding spaces.</span>
      </h1>

      <p class="project-lead">
        What happens when a production RAG system upgrades its embedding
        model, but its historical document vectors cannot be regenerated
        immediately? Drift-RAG investigates whether lightweight,
        query-side alignment can recover retrieval quality while keeping
        historical indexes intact.
      </p>

      <div class="hero-actions">
        <a
          class="button button-primary"
          href={repository}
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >
          View source code <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
        </a>
        <a class="button button-secondary" href="#results">
          Explore results <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span>
        </a>
      </div>

      <div class="metadata" aria-label="Project details">
        <div>
          <span class="meta-label">Project type</span>
          <span>Research & engineering</span>
        </div>
        <div>
          <span class="meta-label">Deployment</span>
          <span>5 virtual machines</span>
        </div>
        <div>
          <span class="meta-label">Core stack</span>
          <span>Scala · Spark · Python · FAISS</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="section-block" aria-labelledby="problem-heading">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">01 / MOTIVATION</p>
        <h2 id="problem-heading">The problem: embedding drift</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="prose reading-width">
        <p>
          A retrieval system encodes documents and queries into vector
          representations. When the embedding model changes, new query
          vectors may no longer align with document vectors produced by
          the older model. Searching the historical index with those
          incompatible vectors can severely degrade retrieval quality.
        </p>
        <p>
          The conventional alternative is to re-embed the historical
          corpus using the new model. For a large, continuously growing
          collection, that operation can be expensive and operationally
          disruptive.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="comparison-grid">
        <article class="comparison-card">
          <span class="comparison-number">01</span>
          <h3>Full re-embedding</h3>
          <p>
            Regenerate document embeddings and rebuild historical indexes
            whenever the model changes.
          </p>
          <span class="comparison-note">High migration cost</span>
        </article>

        <article class="comparison-card comparison-card-featured">
          <span class="comparison-number">02</span>
          <h3>Query-side compensation</h3>
          <p>
            Learn a transformation from a small calibration set and adapt
            new query vectors before searching each historical index.
          </p>
          <span class="comparison-note">The approach we tested</span>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section-block" aria-labelledby="architecture-heading">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">02 / SYSTEM DESIGN</p>
        <h2 id="architecture-heading">A router and three autonomous shards</h2>
        <p class="section-intro">
          The live system runs across five VMs. Each historical-generation
          shard owns its adapter and FAISS index; the router encodes the
          query and fuses the ranked results.
        </p>
      </div>

      <figure class="architecture">
        <div class="architecture-node router-node">
          <span class="node-role">WORKER 1 · ROUTER</span>
          <h3>Query service</h3>
          <p>Encode with BGE-M3 · Dispatch · Fuse results</p>
        </div>

        <div class="flow-label">
          <span class="flow-line" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <span>Raw V3 query embedding</span>
          <span class="flow-line" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        </div>

        <div class="shard-grid">
          <article class="architecture-node shard-node">
            <span class="node-role">WORKER 2</span>
            <h3>Shard · Epoch 1</h3>
            <p class="model-label">BGE-large-en</p>
            <div class="node-component">L1 adapter</div>
            <div class="node-component">FAISS index</div>
          </article>

          <article class="architecture-node shard-node">
            <span class="node-role">WORKER 3</span>
            <h3>Shard · Epoch 2</h3>
            <p class="model-label">BGE-large-en-v1.5</p>
            <div class="node-component">L1 adapter</div>
            <div class="node-component">FAISS index</div>
          </article>

          <article class="architecture-node shard-node">
            <span class="node-role">WORKER 4</span>
            <h3>Shard · Epoch 3</h3>
            <p class="model-label">BGE-M3 · Native space</p>
            <div class="node-component">No adapter needed</div>
            <div class="node-component">FAISS index</div>
          </article>
        </div>

        <div class="flow-label response-flow">
          <span class="flow-line" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <span>Ranked results returned to router for fusion</span>
          <span class="flow-line" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        </div>

        <div class="architecture-node orchestrator-node">
          <span class="node-role">WORKER 5 · ORCHESTRATOR</span>
          <h3>Training and evaluation</h3>
          <p>
            Calibration · Adapter training · Index experiments · Benchmarks
          </p>
        </div>

        <figcaption>
          Conceptual architecture of the deployed system. Query and result
          traffic passes between the router and shards; the orchestrator
          handles training and evaluation rather than serving live queries.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <div class="implementation-notes">
        <div>
          <h3>Offline pipeline</h3>
          <p>
            Spark ingestion and cleaning, chronological corpus partitioning,
            embedding generation, calibration, adapter training, and FAISS
            index construction.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>Live retrieval</h3>
          <p>
            FastAPI services communicate over HTTP. Each shard applies its
            own transformation locally, searches its index, and returns
            ranked results for router-side fusion.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section-block" aria-labelledby="methods-heading">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">03 / COMPENSATION METHODS</p>
        <h2 id="methods-heading">How do we align the embeddings?</h2>
        <p class="section-intro">
          Three adapter designs test whether a simple global transformation
          is sufficient, or whether local corrections provide additional value.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="method-list">
        {methods.map((method) => (
          <article class:list={["method-card", { "method-card-best": method.outcome === "Best overall" }]}>
            <div class="method-heading">
              <h3>{method.name}</h3>
              <span class="method-badge">{method.outcome}</span>
            </div>
            <p>{method.description}</p>
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>

      <div class="equation-panel">
        <p class="equation-label">ORTHOGONAL PROCRUSTES · L1</p>
        <div class="equation" aria-label="Find the orthogonal matrix R that minimizes the Frobenius norm of X R minus Y">
          R* = arg min<sub>R</sub> ‖XR − Y‖<sub>F</sub><sup>2</sup>
          <span class="equation-constraint">subject to RᵀR = I</span>
        </div>
        <p>
          Here, X and Y are paired calibration embeddings from the current
          and historical models. The orthogonality constraint restricts the
          learned transformation to a rotation or reflection, preserving
          vector lengths and inner products within the transformed space.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section-block" id="results" aria-labelledby="results-heading">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">04 / EXPERIMENTAL EVALUATION</p>
        <h2 id="results-heading">Recovering retrieval quality</h2>
        <p class="section-intro">
          The main CC-News evaluation compares unadapted queries,
          compensated queries, and native-model retrieval on the same
          historical-generation task.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="results-summary">
        <article class="result-highlight">
          <span class="result-value">97.6%</span>
          <h3>V3 → V1</h3>
          <p>Oracle NDCG@10 recovered</p>
        </article>
        <article class="result-highlight">
          <span class="result-value">93.5%</span>
          <h3>V3 → V2</h3>
          <p>Oracle NDCG@10 recovered</p>
        </article>
      </div>

      <div class="table-wrapper">
        <table>
          <caption>
            CC-News self-retrieval evaluation · NDCG@10
          </caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Transition</th>
              <th scope="col">Naive</th>
              <th scope="col">L1 compensated</th>
              <th scope="col">Oracle</th>
              <th scope="col">Recovery</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {results.map((result) => (
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">{result.transition}</th>
                <td>{result.naive}</td>
                <td class="best-cell">{result.compensated}</td>
                <td>{result.oracle}</td>
                <td class="best-cell">{result.recovery}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p class="table-note">
        Recovery is calculated as compensated NDCG@10 divided by oracle
        NDCG@10. Values are rounded; percentages use the underlying
        experiment values.
      </p>

      <div class="secondary-evidence">
        <h3>Does the pattern hold on another dataset?</h3>
        <p>
          On NFCorpus, a BEIR benchmark with human relevance judgments,
          compensation also substantially improved retrieval: L1 reached
          approximately 0.276 NDCG@10 against an oracle of 0.359 for V3 → V1,
          and 0.280 against 0.400 for V3 → V2.
        </p>
        <p>
          The recovery percentages from NFCorpus and CC-News should not be
          directly compared. CC-News uses title-to-body self-retrieval,
          while NFCorpus uses graded human relevance judgments.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="negative-findings">
        <p class="eyebrow">WHAT DID NOT WORK?</p>
        <div class="finding-grid">
          <div>
            <h3>Translation alone failed</h3>
            <p>
              L0 remained near zero NDCG@10 on the CC-News historical
              indexes. A global mean shift was insufficient for this
              embedding-model transition.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3>More complexity did not help</h3>
            <p>
              L2 slightly underperformed L1. Cluster-specific residual
              corrections did not improve on the global rotation in these
              experiments.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section-block" aria-labelledby="systems-heading">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">05 / ENGINEERING</p>
        <h2 id="systems-heading">Quality is only part of the system</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="engineering-grid">
        <article class="engineering-card">
          <span class="engineering-number">01</span>
          <h3>Parallel shard requests</h3>
          <p>
            The initial router queried shards sequentially. Switching to
            parallel requests reduced mean end-to-end latency from 331 ms
            to a measured range of approximately 171–242 ms.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="engineering-card">
          <span class="engineering-number">02</span>
          <h3>Exact versus approximate search</h3>
          <p>
            In the reported FAISS comparison, flat search averaged about
            0.82 NDCG@10 and 1.3 ms query latency; IVF at nprobe=16 averaged
            about 0.74 and 0.17 ms. Approximate search traded retrieval
            quality for speed.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="engineering-card">
          <span class="engineering-number">03</span>
          <h3>Calibration instead of migration</h3>
          <p>
            The reported cost baseline estimates 11–15 minutes for
            calibration-only re-embedding, compared with 6.8–9.3 hours
            for full-corpus re-embedding per transition. Full-corpus
            timing is a throughput-based extrapolation.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>

      <div class="stack-panel">
        <p class="eyebrow">TECHNOLOGY STACK</p>
        <div class="stack-tags">
          <span>Scala</span>
          <span>Apache Spark</span>
          <span>Python</span>
          <span>FastAPI</span>
          <span>FAISS</span>
          <span>PyTorch</span>
          <span>Sentence Transformers</span>
          <span>HTTP services</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section-block" aria-labelledby="limitations-heading">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">06 / SCOPE & LIMITATIONS</p>
        <h2 id="limitations-heading">What these results do—and don't—show</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="prose reading-width">
        <p>
          Drift-RAG demonstrates that query-side orthogonal alignment can
          recover much of the measured retrieval quality across the tested
          BGE model transitions. The experiments also show that the
          simplest effective adapter outperformed the more elaborate
          variants considered here.
        </p>
        <p>
          The CC-News evaluation is a constructed self-retrieval task, not
          a general-purpose relevance benchmark. The project also reports
          an unresolved discrepancy: the two transitions rank differently
          under MMD distributional alignment and NDCG-based retrieval
          recovery. These metrics capture related but distinct properties.
        </p>
        <p>
          The implementation returns ranked documents; it does not
          generate answers with an LLM. The reported results are evidence
          for the tested setup, not a guarantee that the same recovery
          will hold for unrelated embedding families, arbitrary corpora,
          or every production workload.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="closing-section">
      <p class="eyebrow">EXPLORE THE IMPLEMENTATION</p>
      <h2>Interested in the system details?</h2>
      <p>
        The repository contains the ingestion pipeline, adapter training,
        distributed retrieval services, and evaluation scripts.
      </p>
      <div class="hero-actions">
        <a
          class="button button-primary"
          href={repository}
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >
          Explore on GitHub <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
        </a>
        <a class="button button-secondary" href="/projects/">
          Back to projects
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
